          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %strong bornological topos
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
            based on the concept of a bounded sequence and was proposed by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} William Lawvere
            in unpublished lectures in 1983 as a convenient category to do
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+analysis"} functional analysis
            in.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            be the category of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+set"} countable sets
            %em bornological topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
            is the category of finite product preserving presheaves on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %h2#properties Properties
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Equivalently,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
                is the topos of sheaves on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                for the
                %em (finite) disjoint covering topology
                with coverings the finite families
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi i
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_i\to Y)_{i\in I}
                , such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi I
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi i
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{i\in I} X_i\to Y
                is an isomorphism.
            %li
              %p
                In the work of Español et al.,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
                is accessed as a subtopos of the topos of actions of the monoid of endomorphisms of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
                contains the category of (Kolmogorov)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bornological+set"} bornological spaces
                as a full reflective subcategory (cf. Español-Lambán
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#EspanolLamban02"} 2002
            %li
              %p
                The object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi R
                        %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_D
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+real+numbers"} Dedekind real numbers
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
                is the space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi l
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l^\infty
                of bounded sequences of real numbers (cf. Español-Mínguez
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#Espanol-Minguez01"} 2001
            %li
              %p
                “
                %em The category of modules over the Dedekind real number object includes all the inductive limits of Banach spaces as a full subcategory, but at the same time is itself a Grothendieck AB5 Abelian category with the accompanying exactness properties.
                ” (Lawvere
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#Lawvere08"} 2008
                %strong functional analysis becomes linear algebra
                ” in the bornological topos (Lawvere
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#Lawvere94"} 1994
          %h2#remark_bornology_and_cohesiveness Remark: bornology and cohesiveness
          %p
            The bornological topos is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gaeta+topos"} Gaeta topos
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            and as such fits Lawvere’s paradigm of doing abstract “algebraic geometry” (cf. Lawvere
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Lawvere86"} 1986
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
            arises
            %em covariantly
            from the basic figure shape of a ‘bounded’ sequence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            via maps
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to X
          %p
            That bornology provides in the context of functional analysis “
            %em> a more basic notion of cohesiveness
            ” than the usual topological neighborhood concept and contravariant function algebra concept based on it is argued in Lawvere (
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere97"}> 1997
            , pp.3f).
          %p
            Although the bornological topos can be regarded as a cohesive category of “spaces” in a broad sense, it doesn’t satisfy Lawvere’s
            %em axiomatic cohesion
            since it lacks the required left adjoint components functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi:\mathcal{B}\to Set
            (cf. Lawvere
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Lawvere08"} 2008
